WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.476
A sports shop buys football boots from a supplier. The supplier's catalogue price is the list price. Because the shop is a trade customer, the supplier takes off a trade discount straight away.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:26.622
Say the list price is two hundred dollars, with ten per cent trade discount. Two hundred minus twenty is one hundred and eighty. That is the net invoice amount, and it is what both businesses record.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:41.288
Cash discount is a reward for paying within a time limit. It is worked out on the net amount, not the list price. Pay in time and you pay one hundred and seventy-one. Pay late and you pay the full one hundred and eighty.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:59.546
Step one, take trade discount off the list price. Step two, check the payment date against the cash discount period. Step three, if it is in time, deduct cash discount from the net amount. If not, pay the net amount in full.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:09.563
To find a rate, divide the discount by the amount it came from, and multiply by one hundred. Thirty dollars off six hundred is five per cent.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:22.879
Here is a real question from June 2023, Paper 1. Calculate the amount paid if payment was made on the third of March 2023. One mark.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:33.264
The invoice is dated the first of March: eighteen bikes at three hundred dollars, ten per cent trade discount, and five per cent cash discount for payment within seven days.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:42.582
Eighteen times three hundred is five thousand four hundred. Trade discount is five hundred and forty. The invoice total is four thousand eight hundred and sixty.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:51.055
Seven days from the first of March means paying by the eighth. The third of March is in time, so cash discount is earned.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:02:07.957
Cash discount is five per cent of four thousand eight hundred and sixty, which is two hundred and forty-three. The amount paid is four thousand six hundred and seventeen. On the ninth, it would be the full four thousand eight hundred and sixty. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:18.563
A common mistake is working out cash discount on the list price. It comes off the net amount, after trade discount.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:30.800
Another is recording the amount after cash discount in the day book. The day book records the net invoice amount. Cash discount is recorded only when payment is made.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:43.774
Now you try. Amara Stores buys tennis rackets. Work out the amount recorded, and the amount paid on each date. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:53.130
Forty times twenty-five is one thousand. Trade discount is two hundred, so the amount recorded is eight hundred dollars.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:03:08.336
Ten days from the second of June is the twelfth. The ninth is in time: two point five per cent of eight hundred is twenty, so Amara Stores pays seven hundred and eighty. The twentieth is late, so it pays eight hundred.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:19.334
Remember: trade discount off the list price first, then check the date, then take cash discount off the net amount only if paid in time. Now try the practice questions.
